<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  macOS Info.plist additions, merged into the bundle by Tauri (and used for
  `tauri dev` on macOS). NSMicrophoneUsageDescription is REQUIRED for the
  voice-notes mic capture (MYND-259): without it, the TCC microphone prompt
  cannot appear and CoreAudio access is denied (often surfacing as silence).
  NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription is the same requirement for the meeting-notes
  system-audio tap (myne-md/myne#789): the CoreAudio process tap needs the
  "System Audio Recording" grant, and Apple's documentation names this key as
  what the prompt reads. Its absence does not fail a build or a test, which is
  why it is written down here.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Myne uses the microphone only when you record a voice note. Audio is encrypted on your device before it is stored.</string>
    <key>NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Myne captures your system's audio only while a meeting session is running, so the other participants are part of the meeting note. Audio is encrypted on your device before it is stored.</string>
    <!--
      Opt out of macOS App Nap. The browser-clipper assembles each clip in the
      Myne WebView (the WebView holds note_create / the importer pipeline); a clip
      arrives while Myne is BACKGROUNDED (the user clicks in their browser). Under
      App Nap, a backgrounded app's run loop is suspended, so the `clipper://dispatch`
      event isn't processed and the relay times out (clip_dispatch_timeout) — clips
      silently fail. Disabling App Nap keeps the WebView responsive so clips assemble
      regardless of focus. (The clip relay is a background-service responsibility of
      the running app; this is the canonical opt-out for such work.)
    -->
    <key>LSAppNapIsDisabled</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
